\section{Analysing the episodes, literately}
\label{app:episodes}

This appendix is the second literate program of the paper: it tangles
to [[analyze_episodes.py]], which computes the quantitative analyses of
\cref{analysis}---the R-SPQ-2F scores (\cref{approach-measure}), the
correlations testing \cref{h:deep,h:success}, the group comparison
testing \cref{h:generative}, and the agreement measures answering
\cref{mq:coding,mq:agreement}.
Presenting the program here also pins down the data formats, which
double as the study's code book.

\subsection{The data files}

The program reads three CSV files, one per data source.

\begin{description}
  \item[{[[rspq.csv]]}] One row per student and administration:
    [[student]], [[administration]] ([[start]] or [[end]],
    \cref{approach-measure,end-measure}), and [[item1]] through
    [[item20]] with the five-point answers.
  \item[{[[episodes.csv]]}] One row per coded debugging episode
    (\cref{analysis}): [[student]], [[episode]], the binary codes
    [[contrast]], [[generalisation]] and [[fusion]], whether the
    episode was [[external]]ly sourced, whether the bug was [[fixed]],
    the number of [[cycles]], the [[seconds]] from first failing to
    first passing run, and the [[coder]]---[[log]] for episodes coded
    from the learnlog data, [[thinkaloud]] for the same episodes coded
    from the think-aloud recordings (\cref{think-aloud}).
  \item[{[[students.csv]]}] One row per student: [[student]], whether
    they are a [[material_user]] (\cref{material-use}), whether they
    have [[prior_experience]] (\cref{prior-knowledge,app:quiz}), and
    the two independent approach classifications, [[interview_class]]
    from the think-aloud accounts and [[quiz_class]] from the coded
    open-text answers ([[deep]], [[surface]], or empty).
\end{description}

\subsection{The program}

The statistical tests come from SciPy; everything else is the standard
library.

